## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of three key concepts: Trie mutations (insert/delete), weighted dynamic programming for word break problems, and the ability to implement both iterative and recursive approaches. It also evaluates understanding of handling dynamic dictionary updates and edge cases such as when no valid decomposition exists.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal weights for efficient prefix matching
- Dynamic insert/delete operations on Trie structure
- Weighted word-break DP with O(n × L) complexity where L = maxWordLen
- Both iterative bottom-up DP and memoized top-down DFS variants

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.endWeight = 0;
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}

class WeightedTrie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.maxWordLen = 0;
  }

  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
    node.endWeight = weight;
    this.maxWordLen = Math.max(this.maxWordLen, word.length);
  }

  delete(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return;
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.isEnd = false;
    node.endWeight = 0;
  }

  maxPartition(s) {
    const n = s.length;
    const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
    dp[n] = 0;

    for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      let node = this.root;
      for (let j = i; j < Math.min(n, i + this.maxWordLen); j++) {
        const char = s[j];
        if (!node.children.has(char)) break;
        node = node.children.get(char);
        if (node.isEnd && dp[j + 1] !== -1) {
          dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], node.endWeight + dp[j + 1]);
        }
      }
    }
    return dp[0];
  }

  maxPartitionDFS(s, memo = {}) {
    if (s.length === 0) return 0;
    if (s in memo) return memo[s];

    let maxWeight = -1;
    let node = this.root;

    for (let i = 0; i < Math.min(s.length, this.maxWordLen); i++) {
      const char = s[i];
      if (!node.children.has(char)) break;
      node = node.children.get(char);

      if (node.isEnd) {
        const remaining = this.maxPartitionDFS(s.slice(i + 1), memo);
        if (remaining !== -1) {
          maxWeight = Math.max(maxWeight, node.endWeight + remaining);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[s] = maxWeight;
    return maxWeight;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests adaptive sorting algorithms, merge logic, and the ability to reason about time complexity in practical, maintainable ways.

## Key Concepts

- **Adaptive sorting**: Using a pre-check to achieve O(n) best-case performance when the array is already sorted
- **Iterative bottom-up mergesort**: Avoiding recursion by iteratively merging sorted subarrays of increasing size
- **Stability and memory usage**: Understanding the trade-offs of O(n) auxiliary space for guaranteed stability

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortWithEarlyMerge(arr) {
  // Early return if already sorted
  for (let i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] < arr[i - 1]) break;
    if (i === arr.length - 1) return arr;
  }

  // Iterative bottom-up mergesort
  const aux = [...arr];
  let width = 1;

  while (width < arr.length) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += width * 2) {
      const left = i;
      const mid = Math.min(i + width, arr.length);
      const right = Math.min(i + width * 2, arr.length);

      let p = left, q = mid, k = left;
      while (p < mid && q < right) {
        aux[k++] = arr[p] <= arr[q] ? arr[p++] : arr[q++];
      }
      while (p < mid) aux[k++] = arr[p++];
      while (q < right) aux[k++] = arr[q++];
    }

    [arr, aux] = [aux, arr];
    width *= 2;
  }

  return arr;
}
```

## Time Complexity Analysis

- **Best case (O(n))**: Array is already sorted; single linear scan confirms this
- **Worst case (O(n log n))**: Array is reverse sorted; full mergesort required
- **Space complexity**: O(n) for the auxiliary buffer

